<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.14.5: Writing an Equation of a Line Parallel or Perpendicular to an Axis</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00300</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.14.5: Writing an Equation of a Line Parallel or Perpendicular to an Axis</md:title>
    <md:uuid>136d2525-8650-4695-b2da-baff3d032e5c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (18 minutes)</title>
<para>Students have worked on finding lines that are parallel and perpendicular to other lines that have slopes that are defined and non-zero. In this activity, students will work to find lines that are parallel and perpendicular to the \( x \)- and \( y \)-axes. To do this, students must have an understanding of the coordinate system. They must understand that the \(y\)-axis is vertical and the \(x\)-axis is horizontal. They must also understand that a coordinate is written in the form \( (x, y) \).</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Put students in pairs to work on this activity. Give each student a coordinate grid. A straight-edge and colored pencils would also help students visualize the different types of lines and their relationships.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>The line \( x = 5 \) and the point \( (3, -2) \) are shown on the graph.</para>
<figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6ab4e4d44454ea9de34c2ec04510c0d814f5dde7"/></media></figure><para>Work with a partner to complete the tasks:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Identify the slope of the original line, \( l \).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Undefined</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph line 
    m through the point \((3, -2)\) and parallel to line \(l\) using the Desmos tool below. </item>
</list>
<para>(Students were provided access to Desmos.)</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 
  <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/762f45c5cbd883257d776bc76f6454e4ad8409f3"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Then,</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Identify the slope of the new line, \( m \). </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Undefined</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write the equation of the new line, \( m \).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( x = 3 \)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph line 
    \(n\) through the point \((3, -2)\) and perpendicular to line \(l\) using the Desmos tool below.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 
  <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e594bdd5cfed921499039c666e33efea172080ba"/></media></figure></para>
<para>(Students were provided access to Desmos.)</para>
<para>Then,</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Identify the slope of the new line, \( n \). </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 0</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write the equation of the new line, \( n \).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( y = -2 \)</para>

<para>The original line \(l\) and the line \(m\) that you created are both parallel to the \(y\)-axis and perpendicular to the \(x\)-axis. It follows, then, that line n is parallel to the \(x\)-axis and perpendicular to the \(y\)-axis.</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write the equation of a line that is parallel to the \(y\)-axis and passing through \((-2, -5)\).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( x = -2 \)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write the equation of a line that is perpendicular to the \(y\)-axis and passing through \((1, 5)\).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( y = 5 \)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write the equation of a line parallel to the \(x\)-axis and passing through \((3, 4)\). </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( y = 4 \)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="11" type="enumerated">
  <item>How does the line described as “parallel to the \(x\)-axis and passing through \((3, 4)\)” relate to the \(y\)-axis? Will it always, sometimes, or never be perpendicular to it?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Always perpendicular</para>


<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>The purpose of the discussion is to elicit conversation about slopes of horizontal lines, vertical lines, parallel lines, and perpendicular lines. Here are some questions for discussion:</para>
<list>
  <item>“How did you find the slope of line \(l\)?” (Vertical lines have undefined slope.)</item>
  <item>“How does the slope of line \(m\) compare to line \(l\)?” (They are the same because the lines are parallel.)</item>
  <item>“How does the slope of line \(n\) compare to line \(l\)?” (The slope of line \(n\) is zero because it is a horizontal line. The slope of line \(l\) is undefined because it is a vertical line.)</item>
  <item>“How do you write the equation of a horizontal line with coordinates \((a, b)\)?” \((y = b)\)</item>
  <item>“How do you write the equation of a vertical line with coordinates \((a, b)\)?” \((x = a)\)</item>
</list>


<title>1.14.5: Self Check </title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Which is the equation of a line that is perpendicular to the line \( x = 1 \) that contains the point \( (-5, 2) \)?</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answers</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>\( y = 2  \) </entry>
      <entry><para>That’s correct! Check yourself: The given line is vertical and has undefined slope. </para>
        <para>The perpendicular line is horizontal and has slope 0. Every point on the perpendicular line has a \( y \)-coordinate of 2. </para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\( x = -5 \)</entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The line you chose is parallel, not perpendicular. The given line is vertical and has undefined slope. </para>
        <para>The perpendicular line is horizontal and has slope 0. Every point on the perpendicular line has a \( y \)-coordinate of 2. The answer is \( y = 2 \).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\( y = 1 \)</entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The line you chose does not go through the point \( (-5, 2) \). The given line is vertical and has undefined slope. </para>
        <para>The perpendicular line is horizontal and has slope 0. Every point on the perpendicular line has a \( y \)-coordinate of 2. The answer is \( y = 2 \).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\( y = -2  \)</entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The line you chose does not go through the point \( (-5, 2) \). The given line is vertical and has undefined slope. </para>
        <para>The perpendicular line is horizontal and has slope 0. Every point on the perpendicular line has a \( y \)-coordinate of 2. The answer is \( y = 2 \).</para></entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>1.14.5: Additional Resources </title>
<para>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</para>

<title>Find Equations of Lines Perpendicular to an Axis</title>

<para>Lines that are horizontal or vertical are special for two reasons. First, their equations have just one variable.</para>
<list> 
  <item>Horizontal line \( y = b \); because all the \( y \)-coordinates are the same and the slope equals zero.</item>
  <item>Vertical line \( x = a \); because all the \( x \)-coordinates are the same and the slope is undefined.</item>  
</list>

<para>Second, they have a special relationship with the x- and y-axes because they are either parallel or perpendicular to them.</para>

<para>Examine the line that passes through \((0, 4)\) and \((3, 4)\).</para>

<para><figure><media alt="Line passing through (0,4) and (3,4)"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c886e626d317cba95270bbbc6e0f4d5be8147467"/></media></figure></para>

<para><emphasis>What is the rise?</emphasis> The rise is 0.
<emphasis>What is the run?</emphasis> The run is 3.
<emphasis>What is the slope?</emphasis> \( m = \frac{\text{rise}}{\text{run}} = \frac{0}{3} = 0 \)</para>

<para>So, the horizontal line passing through \((0, 4)\) and \((3, 4)\) has a slope of 0. For all of the points on this line, the \( y \)-values will always equal 4. So, the equation is \( y = 4 \).</para>

<para>Also notice that it is parallel to the \(x\)-axis and perpendicular to the \(y\)-axis.</para>

<para>Now consider the vertical line passing through \((3, 2)\) and \((3, 0)\).</para>

<para><figure><media alt="Line passing through (3,2) and (3,0)"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/69272e3b11c1d2c6fdc20ad28d4461233df12159"/></media></figure></para>

<para><emphasis>What is the rise?</emphasis> The rise is 2.
<emphasis>What is the run?</emphasis> The run is 0.
<emphasis>What is the slope?</emphasis> \( m = \frac{\text{rise}}{\text{run}} = \frac{2}{0} \) (undefined)</para>

<para>So, the vertical line passing through \((3, 2)\) and \((3, 0)\) has an undefined slope. And, for all of the points on this line, the \( x \)-values will always equal 3. So, the equation is \( x = 3 \).</para>

<para>Also notice that it is parallel to the \(y\)-axis and perpendicular to the \(x\)-axis.</para>

<para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
<para>Find the equation of the line parallel to the \(x\)-axis and passing through \((8, 1)\).</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Identify the point.
\((8, 1)\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the slope of the line.
Since the line is parallel to the \(x\)-axis, the line is horizontal. This means the slope of the line is 0.
\( m = 0 \)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into an equation.
From here, you may know the equation, but if you don’t, then we can use the point-slope equation.
\( y - y_1 = m(x - x_1) \)
\( y - 1 = 0(x - 8) \)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.
\( y - 1 = 0 \)
\( y = 1 \)</para>

<para>Thus, the equation of the line parallel to the \(x\)-axis and passing through \((8, 1)\) is \( y = 1 \).</para>

<para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
<para>Find the equation of the line perpendicular to the \(x\)-axis and passing through \((8, 1)\).</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Identify the point.
\((8, 1)\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the slope of the line.
Since the line is perpendicular to the \(x\)-axis, the line is vertical. This means the slope of the line is undefined!
\( m = \text{undefined} \)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into an equation.
Since the slope is undefined, we cannot use the point-slope equation. But, we know that if \((8, 1)\) is on the line, then another point vertically above this point is \((8, 2)\). Another point vertically below this point is \((8, 0)\). On a vertical line, the x-values do not change.
So, \( x = x_1 \)
\( x = 8 \)</para>


<para><emphasis>Example 3</emphasis></para>
<para>Find an equation of a line perpendicular to \( x=5 \) that contains the point \( (3,-2) \). Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
<para>Again, since we know one point, the point-slope option seems more promising than the slope-intercept option. We need the slope to use this form, and we know the new line will be perpendicular to \( x=5 \). This line is vertical, so its perpendicular will be horizontal. This tells us the \( m_{\perp}=0 \).
</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Identify the point.
  \( (3,-2) \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the slope of the perpendicular line.
  \( m_{\perp}=0 \) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into an equation.
  \( \begin{array}{rcl}y-y_1&amp;=&amp;m(x-x_1) \\  y-(-2)&amp;=&amp;0(x-3)\end{array} \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify
  \( \begin{array}{rcl}y+2&amp;=&amp;0 \\ y&amp;=&amp;-2\end{array} \)</para>

<para><emphasis>Example 4</emphasis></para>
<para>Find an equation of a line that is perpendicular to \( y=4 \) that contains the point \( (2,3) \). Write the equation in slope-intercept form.</para>
<para>The line \( y=4 \) is a horizontal line. Any line perpendicular to it must be vertical, in the form \( x=a \). Since the perpendicular line is vertical and passes through \( (2,3) \), every point on it has an \(x\)-coordinate of 2. The equation of the perpendicular line is \( x=2 \).</para>
<title>Try It: Find Equations of Lines Perpendicular to an Axis</title>
<para>Find an equation of a line that is perpendicular to \( y = -3 \) that contains the point \( (-3, 5) \).</para>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>Here is how to find the equation of a line perpendicular to \( y = -3 \) that contains the point \( (-3, 5) \).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope of the given line. 
  The line is horizontal.
  \( m = 0 \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the slope of the perpendicular line.
  The perpendicular line is a vertical line.
  undefined</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Identify the point.
  The given point is \((-3, 5)\).  
  \( (x_1, y_1) \)
  \( (-3, 5) \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Write the equation.
  The perpendicular line is vertical and passes through \( (-3, 5) \); every point on it has an \(x\)-coordinate of \( -3 \). 
  \( x = -3 \)</para>
</section>
</content>
